                                                                                                                <comments>
                            <comment id="15433255" author="pnarang" created="Tue, 23 Aug 2016 17:36:21 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=andrew_duffy&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;andrew_duffy&quot;&gt;andrew_duffy&lt;/a&gt; do you have a link to the corresponding avro / spark code handy? That would make fixing this much easier. &lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15433275" author="pnarang" created="Tue, 23 Aug 2016 17:45:47 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Umm nevermind, just saw your PR &lt;img class=&quot;emoticon&quot; src=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/images/icons/emoticons/smile.png&quot; height=&quot;16&quot; width=&quot;16&quot; align=&quot;absmiddle&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; border=&quot;0&quot;/&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15854487" author="lian cheng" created="Mon, 6 Feb 2017 18:22:46 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=rdblue&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;rdblue&quot;&gt;rdblue&lt;/a&gt;, shall we resolve this since you&apos;ve fixed it in &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/apache/parquet-mr/pull/367&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;PR #367&lt;/a&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15854599" author="rdblue" created="Mon, 6 Feb 2017 19:36:07 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;We shouldn&apos;t close this yet because we&apos;re working on adding sort order to the format. See the new link to Format PR #46.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15854685" author="julianhyde" created="Mon, 6 Feb 2017 20:31:02 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;It seems to me that the correct way to represent string orderings is to adhere to the same specification as  the COLLATE in a standard SQL data type.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quoting to &lt;a href=&quot;http://stackoverflow.com/questions/5039211/what-does-collate-sql-latin1-general-cp1-ci-as-do&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;a stackoverflow question about the COLLATE clause in Microsoft SQL Server&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;tt&gt;SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS&lt;/tt&gt;&lt;br/&gt;
breaks up into interesting parts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;tt&gt;latin1&lt;/tt&gt; makes the server treat strings using charset latin 1, basically ascii&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;tt&gt;CI&lt;/tt&gt; case insensitive comparisons so &apos;ABC&apos; would equal &apos;abc&apos;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;tt&gt;AS&lt;/tt&gt; accent sensitive, so &apos;&#252;&apos; does not equal &apos;u&apos;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;I couldn&apos;t find an ISO definition of collation, but several databases support it, including &lt;a href=&quot;https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.7/en/charset-collate.html&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;MySQL&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.oracle.com/database/122/SQLRF/COLLATE-Operator.htm&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;Oracle 12&lt;/a&gt;, so I suspect it is pretty well defined.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15854711" author="rdblue" created="Mon, 6 Feb 2017 20:47:03 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=julianhyde&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;julianhyde&quot;&gt;julianhyde&lt;/a&gt;, I&apos;m not sure that we should make this SQL-specific. Right now, the PR allows a String identifier for custom sort orders, so this example could be expressed as &quot;sql:SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS&quot;. Does that work?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also, I&apos;d like to find a spec for these, even if it is just a page documenting what postgres or some other implementation does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;*&lt;b&gt;Edit&lt;/b&gt;*: Fixed the example, since &lt;tt&gt;SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS&lt;/tt&gt; is one collating sequence&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15854720" author="julianhyde" created="Mon, 6 Feb 2017 20:51:55 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;If you&apos;re following the relevant industry standard you&apos;re not being SQL specific. I don&apos;t know whether there is an ISO standard, but even if there isn&apos;t, we&apos;d be well advised to follow what the big DBs do. The folks at SQL Server and Oracle have had more time to identify the appropriate standard to follow. If we roll our own standard we are very likely to come up with something worse.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15854825" author="rdblue" created="Mon, 6 Feb 2017 21:57:57 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;The standard I found is ICU 54: &lt;a href=&quot;http://userguide.icu-project.org/collation/api#TOC-Instantiating-the-Predefined-Collators&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;http://userguide.icu-project.org/collation/api#TOC-Instantiating-the-Predefined-Collators&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I agree that we should support what SQL engines do, but I don&apos;t agree that they&apos;ve necessarily chosen an appropriate or &quot;correct&quot; one. What they do is widely used enough to be a de-facto standard, but I don&apos;t think that should preclude supporting other standards.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15854888" author="julianhyde" created="Mon, 6 Feb 2017 22:41:09 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;In Calcite, a collation is a character set, a dollar separator, a locale, a dollar separator, and a strength. Thus: &lt;tt&gt;COLLATE &quot;ISO-8859-1$en_US$primary&quot;&lt;/tt&gt;. I have not had time to check how/whether that jibes with the SQL standard. It does seem to align somewhat with &lt;a href=&quot;http://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/text/Collator.html&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;java.text.Collator&lt;/a&gt;, which has locale and strength.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="16041802" author="julienledem" created="Wed, 7 Jun 2017 22:38:56 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;The first issue of not returning bad stats was solved in: &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/apache/parquet-mr/commit/de99127d77dabfc6c8134b3c58e0b9a0b74e5f37&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://github.com/apache/parquet-mr/commit/de99127d77dabfc6c8134b3c58e0b9a0b74e5f37&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="16055749" author="zi" created="Tue, 20 Jun 2017 13:25:48 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/pull/46&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;PR #46&lt;/a&gt; went through a lot of changes during the code review and ended up being entirely different than the original thinking. However, when it got committed, some parts of it still contained references to the original solution that got abandoned. I created &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/pull/55&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;PR #55&lt;/a&gt; to clean up these inconsistencies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Can I reopen this issue for this change or should I create a new one for these clarifications?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="16195435" author="rdblue" created="Fri, 6 Oct 2017 23:39:23 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Merged PR #55 with some follow-ups. Thanks for the fix, &lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=zi&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;zi&quot;&gt;zi&lt;/a&gt;!&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="16248231" author="igozali" created="Sat, 11 Nov 2017 00:39:11 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Hi all, just curious if this issue is also resolved in Parquet 1.8.2, which is what Spark 2.2.0 uses?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="17857647" author="rok" created="Sun, 23 Jun 2024 03:28:34 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;This issue has been migrated to &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/issues/314&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;issue #314&lt;/a&gt; on GitHub. Please see the &lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/PARQUET-2502&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot;&gt;migration documentation&lt;/a&gt; for further details.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
